AI and LLM APIs
Anthropic Claude API is your primary LLM for summarisation, change narration, and report generation. You'll use claude-opus-4 for complex reasoning tasks and claude-haiku for fast, cheap bulk passes. Pricing is per token — budget roughly ₹2,000–8,000 per month depending on volume during development. For the on-premises Stage 2 round, have an offline fallback ready.
Ollama is that offline fallback. It runs Llama 3, Mistral, and other open-source models entirely on your own hardware. During the CDSCO on-premises round you may not have internet access, so having Ollama running with Llama 3.1 8B means your summarisation and narration modules keep working without Claude.
HuggingFace Hub is where you pull pre-trained models from — the BERT model for SAE classification, sentence-transformer models for embeddings, spaCy models. Free to use, just needs internet at model download time. After downloading, models run locally.
OpenAI API is optional but useful as a fallback if Claude is unavailable. Not required if you have Ollama as your offline fallback.

Document Processing
PyMuPDF (fitz) — free, open-source Python library. Your primary tool for extracting native text, tables, and images from PDFs. No API key needed.
Tesseract OCR — free, open-source. Google's OCR engine for scanned pages. Install on the server, call it from Python via pytesseract. Supports Hindi and other Indian languages out of the box.
Apache Tika — free, open-source Java-based tool that extracts text from virtually any file format. Your safety net for unusual formats like older DOCX versions, RTF, or proprietary formats some applicants might submit.
Camelot or pdfplumber — free Python libraries specifically for extracting tables from PDFs. PyMuPDF handles basic tables but these handle complex multi-column regulatory tables much better.

NLP and Machine Learning
spaCy — free, open-source. The NLP backbone for named entity recognition. Download the en_core_web_lg model for best accuracy.
Microsoft Presidio — free, open-source. Your PII detection and anonymisation engine. Built specifically for this use case, maintained by Microsoft. No API key needed.
HuggingFace Transformers — free, open-source Python library. Used to load, fine-tune, and run BERT and other transformer models for your SAE classifier.
sentence-transformers — free, open-source. Generates the embeddings you store in ChromaDB. The all-mpnet-base-v2 model is your best general-purpose choice.
FAISS — free, open-source from Meta. Your vector similarity engine for duplicate detection. Runs in-memory, extremely fast.
LangChain — free, open-source. Your orchestration framework that chains together LLM calls, retrieval, and tool use. This is what coordinates the four modules.

Infrastructure and Backend
FastAPI — free, open-source Python web framework. Your entire backend API is built on this.
Apache Kafka — free, open-source. Your message bus. For the hackathon, you can run a single-node Kafka instance on Docker. For production, use a three-node cluster.
Redis — free, open-source. Caching layer for session state, rate limiting, and short-lived queue state.
PostgreSQL — free, open-source. Your primary relational database.
MinIO — free, open-source. S3-compatible object storage that runs on your own servers. Critical for the on-premises Stage 2 round since you won't be pushing patient documents to AWS.
ChromaDB — free, open-source. Your vector database for embeddings and semantic search.
Docker and Docker Compose — free. Every service runs in a container. You can spin the entire platform on any machine with one command.
Kubernetes (K8s) — free, open-source. For production deployment and scaling. For the hackathon demo, Docker Compose is sufficient. Mention K8s in your architecture presentation to show production readiness.
Nginx — free, open-source. Reverse proxy sitting in front of all your FastAPI services. Handles SSL termination, load balancing, and rate limiting at the network level.

Frontend
React — free, open-source. Your reviewer dashboard UI.
TanStack Query (React Query) — free, open-source. Handles all API calls, caching, and loading states in the dashboard.
Recharts or Apache ECharts — free, open-source. For analytics charts on the dashboard — submission volumes, SAE trends, completeness score distributions.
WeasyPrint — free, open-source Python library. Converts your HTML report templates to polished PDFs. This is how you generate CDSCO-format inspection reports.

Security
Keycloak — free, open-source identity and access management. Handles login, JWT issuance, role-based access control, and MFA. This is enterprise-grade auth without writing auth code yourself. CDSCO reviewers, SUGAM operators, and admin staff all get different roles and permissions.
HashiCorp Vault — free, open-source. Stores all secrets — API keys, database passwords, encryption keys. Your application never has secrets in environment variables or config files. Everything is pulled from Vault at runtime. Auditors love this.
Let's Encrypt via Certbot — free. TLS certificates for all your HTTPS endpoints.

Monitoring and Observability
Prometheus — free, open-source. Scrapes metrics from all your services — request counts, latency, error rates, model inference times.
Grafana — free, open-source. Dashboards on top of Prometheus data. You want one dashboard per module showing throughput, error rate, and AI confidence score distributions.
ELK Stack (Elasticsearch + Logstash + Kibana) — free, open-source. Centralised logging across all services. When something breaks at 2am, you find the root cause in minutes instead of hours.
Sentry — free tier available, paid for higher volume. Catches and groups application errors in real time, with full stack traces. The free tier is sufficient for the hackathon.

Government and Regulatory APIs
SUGAM Portal API — provided by CDSCO. You'll need to formally request API credentials from the hackathon organisers. This is the integration that makes your solution real rather than simulated.
MD Online Portal API — same as above, also from CDSCO. Request both together.
DigiLocker API — free, government-provided. Lets applicants submit verified documents directly from their DigiLocker account. Adds legitimacy to your document authenticity chain and aligns with Digital India initiative, which judges will notice.
Aadhaar Offline KYC API (via UIDAI) — free for government use cases. For verifying applicant identity during submission without storing Aadhaar numbers. You store only the verification token, not the Aadhaar itself — this is the DPDP-compliant way.
India Post Pincode API — free, public. Validates address fields in submissions. Tiny detail but eliminates a class of data quality issues.

Communication and Notifications
Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM) — free tier is generous. Push notifications to the mobile reviewer app when a high-priority SAE comes in.
Twilio or MSG91 — MSG91 is the Indian alternative and cheaper for Indian SMS. For SMS alerts to reviewers on critical cases. MSG91 has a free trial, then roughly ₹0.15–0.20 per SMS.
SendGrid or AWS SES — for email notifications. SendGrid free tier gives 100 emails/day which is fine for development. AWS SES is cheaper at scale — roughly ₹70 per 10,000 emails.

Training Data and Fine-tuning
PubMed API — free, provided by NIH. Gives you access to millions of biomedical abstracts. Use this to build a domain-specific corpus for fine-tuning your embedding model and BERT classifier.
CDSCO Public Guidance Documents — free, available on the CDSCO website. Parse these PDFs to build your rules engine and to teach your LLM about Indian regulatory terminology.
Weights and Biases (W&B) — free tier available. Tracks your model training experiments — loss curves, accuracy metrics, hyperparameter comparisons. When you fine-tune the SAE BERT classifier, you use W&B to know which training run actually improved things.
Google Colab or Kaggle Notebooks — free GPU access for initial model fine-tuning. Once you have a trained model, export it and run it locally.
